THE NOTATION
------------

Four glyphs. Two statuses, and a dot meaning change is happening.

    O       open
    |       closed
    (.)     open, and changing
    |.|     closed, and changing      (a vertical line with a dot at its middle)

The dot in the centre is the mark of alteration. It is drawn on whichever status
the actor is in, so the glyph says both what a thing is and whether it is moving.


WHAT REPLACED THE EVENT SYSTEM
------------------------------

The old design: ~2,000 hand-written block facts, plus 20,000-40,000 house facts
priced in its own document at two to four years of typing. A design that states
that number about itself has been refuted by its own estimate.

What is there instead:

  An event is an occurrence, not a possession. It is a crossing -- two actors in
  one place at one hour. Nothing is stored; it is computed by evaluating two
  whereabouts equations at the same hour.

  Character is a sparse map from axis-name to a value. Arbitrarily many axes, and
  they are minted on demand as a place develops a nature. Most places carry few.

  A place's character is the characters of the people who visit it, plus a little
  of its own natural character. Natural character is physical and does not move:
  on the water, in the willow's shadow, backed onto the wall, steep, stone.

  An axis and a filter are the same object. Doc 006 defines a filter as name,
  colour, angle, mode, and a reading of (person, place) -> a number or nothing.
  An axis minted on demand is exactly that record. So the filter list is not
  authored; it grows as the city grows axes.

  "Nothing" stops being a special case and becomes structural. Bare painting is
  a place that has not grown that axis at all -- not missing data.


THE CROSSING TABLE
------------------

The outcome of a crossing is dispatched on the two actors' statuses. Four cells,
which is a dispatch table rather than a staircase of branches.

    a         b         what happens
    -------   -------   -----------------------------------------------
    open      open      both receive, neither gives -- see below
    open      closed    the closed gives, the open receives. clean transfer.
    closed    open      the same, mirrored.
    closed    closed    conflict -- and conflict can be productive.
                        "two closed hands grasped around one another,
                        lifting to see the view atop the roof."

CORRECTED BY THE AUTHOR: open does not mean participating in exchange. It means
OPEN TO BEING CHANGED. Things are always exchanging. The closed gives, the open
receives. Exchange is unconditional; the statuses decide only which way it runs.


THE IMPACT RATIO
----------------

    the ratio between the total time in the day and the "open" times determines
    how impactful each specific situation is

Impact is inverse to how much of the day an actor spends open. Someone open for
two hours out of twenty-four has each of those two hours land hard. Someone open
most of the day is changed by nothing in particular.

The humane reading, and the one that fits the vision's complaint about rigid
lives: the labourer whose day is pinned to work has almost no open hours, and so
the few they have matter enormously.


FORCING -- THE SECOND WAY AN AXIS IS MINTED
-------------------------------------------

The first way is an unlike meeting: two actors who differ enough cross, and the
difference gets named.

The second is force. A closed thing can be made open by an act done to it, and
the forcing itself mints an axis. The author's example is a sacred grove burned
down -- the grove is closed, the burning forces it open, and it comes out of that
carrying an axis it did not have.

The name of that axis is generated on the fly, and which word is chosen matters:

    it applies an axis to it that might be "ashen" or "consumed" - both of those
    reflect a different spirit, and the spirit is generated on-the-fly because we
    never know how something will be until we mix it up and see.

So the word is not a label on a number. The word is the thing. Two names for the
same burning are two different spirits, and which one arrives is not knowable in
advance -- it comes out of the mixing.


A VOCABULARY COLLISION TO RESOLVE
---------------------------------

Doc 001 says every word in this project means exactly one thing. "Open" now has
two candidate meanings and they disagree in the documents:

  Doc 003, on physical access:  "Mostly open: it is rare for a building to be
                                 closed to people, and the largest are entirely
                                 free to enter."
  This note, on receptivity:     "Most buildings are closed, all the time."

The reconciliation that seems right: these are different properties wearing one
word. A building can be free to walk into and utterly closed to change. The
colonnade admits anyone and takes on nothing. Physical access is not receptivity.

If that is right, one of the two needs a different word.


